1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,847
A radio wave is how a message crosses empty space with nothing to carry it.

2
00:00:06,847 --> 00:00:12,212
No wire, no air required — a police call, a broadcast, a satellite picture all ride

3
00:00:12,212 --> 00:00:13,892
the same physical vehicle.

4
00:00:13,892 --> 00:00:19,192
Formally: a radio wave is electromagnetic radiation in the radio-frequency range —

5
00:00:19,192 --> 00:00:24,169
about three kilohertz to three hundred gigahertz — travelling at the speed of

6
00:00:24,169 --> 00:00:29,598
light, and it carries information when its amplitude, frequency, or phase is varied.

7
00:00:29,598 --> 00:00:34,316
That varying has a name — modulation — and it's the whole trick of radio.

8
00:00:34,766 --> 00:00:39,924
The everyday anchor is a ripple on a pond: a disturbance spreading outward in rings

9
00:00:39,924 --> 00:00:41,788
from where the energy went in.

10
00:00:41,788 --> 00:00:46,573
But here's where the analogy deliberately breaks — a pond ripple needs water.

11
00:00:46,573 --> 00:00:48,810
A radio wave needs no medium at all.

12
00:00:48,810 --> 00:00:53,844
It carries its own support with it, which is exactly why radio crosses the vacuum

13
00:00:53,844 --> 00:00:54,404
of space.

14
00:00:54,854 --> 00:00:56,415
How does it carry itself?

15
00:00:56,415 --> 00:01:00,162
A transmitter drives an alternating current into an antenna.

16
00:01:00,162 --> 00:01:05,159
Those accelerating charges launch a self-propagating disturbance: an oscillating

17
00:01:05,159 --> 00:01:09,843
electric field regenerates a magnetic field at right angles to it, and that

18
00:01:09,843 --> 00:01:14,902
magnetic field in turn regenerates the electric field — so the pair detaches from

19
00:01:14,902 --> 00:01:18,337
the antenna and radiates outward at the speed of light.

20
00:01:18,337 --> 00:01:20,960
Each field continually rebuilds the other.

21
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:23,645
That self-sustaining handshake is the wave.

22
00:01:24,095 --> 00:01:29,350
And a simple radio wave is fully described by just three properties — the only

23
00:01:29,350 --> 00:01:31,775
things a transmitter can manipulate.

24
00:01:31,775 --> 00:01:33,392
Amplitude: its strength.

25
00:01:33,392 --> 00:01:35,616
Frequency: its cycles per second.

26
00:01:35,616 --> 00:01:38,108
Phase: its position within the cycle.

27
00:01:38,108 --> 00:01:43,430
Every modulation scheme ever invented is a way of wiggling one or more of those

28
00:01:43,430 --> 00:01:46,529
three in step with the information being sent.

29
00:01:46,529 --> 00:01:47,338
Three knobs.

30
00:01:47,338 --> 00:01:49,291
That's the entire vocabulary.

31
00:01:49,741 --> 00:01:55,418
The numbers worth keeping: in a vacuum the wave moves at about 299,792,458 metres

32
00:01:55,418 --> 00:02:01,166
per second — the speed of light — and its wavelength follows from its frequency by

33
00:02:01,166 --> 00:02:02,778
lambda equals c over f.

34
00:02:02,778 --> 00:02:04,109
And at the far end?

35
00:02:04,109 --> 00:02:09,927
A distant antenna, immersed in the passing field, develops a tiny induced current —

36
00:02:09,927 --> 00:02:15,604
often just a few microvolts — which the receiver amplifies, filters, and decodes.

37
00:02:15,604 --> 00:02:20,019
Radio is the art of doing something useful with almost nothing.

38
00:02:20,019 --> 00:02:25,626
One practical footnote rides along: the wave's polarization — the orientation of

39
00:02:25,626 --> 00:02:31,373
that electric field — must usually match between the transmit and receive antennas

40
00:02:31,373 --> 00:02:36,139
for good reception, which is why antenna orientation isn't cosmetic.

41
00:02:36,589 --> 00:02:38,310
Why care about the physics?

42
00:02:38,310 --> 00:02:40,286
Because the journey is hostile.

43
00:02:40,286 --> 00:02:45,258
Even in free space the wave's power density falls with the square of distance.

44
00:02:45,258 --> 00:02:50,165
Lower-frequency waves diffract around hills and buildings; higher frequencies

45
00:02:50,165 --> 00:02:54,818
travel more like light and get blocked or reflected, producing multipath.

46
00:02:54,818 --> 00:02:59,917
And the wave always arrives buried in noise, thermal and man-made — the ratio of

47
00:02:59,917 --> 00:03:04,698
wanted signal to that floor is the signal-to-noise ratio, and it bounds how

48
00:03:04,698 --> 00:03:07,439
reliably the message can be decoded at all.

49
00:03:07,439 --> 00:03:12,219
Every receiver ever built is fighting that same three-front war: spreading,

50
00:03:12,219 --> 00:03:13,303
obstacles, noise.

51
00:03:13,753 --> 00:03:19,101
By the time GopherTrunk sees a radio wave, its front end has mixed it down and the

52
00:03:19,101 --> 00:03:24,385
converter has turned it into IQ samples — complex numbers capturing the amplitude

53
00:03:24,385 --> 00:03:26,864
and phase of the wave at each instant.

54
00:03:26,864 --> 00:03:31,951
Watch the spectrum and waterfall view and the signal meter in dBFS: that's the

55
00:03:31,951 --> 00:03:33,908
physical wave, become numbers.

56
00:03:33,908 --> 00:03:39,257
Every property you just met survives the conversion, and every one must be tracked

57
00:03:39,257 --> 00:03:40,757
to recover the message.

58
00:03:41,207 --> 00:03:46,415
So: a radio wave is two fields carrying each other through nothing, at light speed,

59
00:03:46,415 --> 00:03:51,623
with exactly three properties a transmitter can vary — and everything else in radio

60
00:03:51,623 --> 00:03:53,129
is built on those three.

61
00:03:53,129 --> 00:03:54,823
Full write-up linked below.

